module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.MvPowerSeries.Inverse
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Noetherian.Defs
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.RingHom.Flat
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.AtPrime.Basic
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Flat.FaithfullyFlat.Basic
import Mathlib.RingTheory.MvPowerSeries.Equiv
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Flat.FaithfullyFlat.Algebra
import WeilConjectures.RingTheory.Flat.FlatModulePowers

/-! # Flat coefficientwise extension of formal power series

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 0523 (lemma-flat-module-powers), specialized to
formal variables, with Tags 0306, 00HT and 00HR for the local corollary
(blueprint: lem-per-formal-coefficient-taylor-faithful-flatness).

## Main definitions

* `MvPowerSeries.localCoefficientMap`: the canonical map from the localization at the
  inverse image of the target maximal ideal to the local target power-series ring.

## Main statements

* `MvPowerSeries.map_flat_and_local_faithfullyFlat`: coefficientwise flatness and the
  faithfully flat local form, including zero variables.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

namespace MvPowerSeries

/-- The canonical localization of the coefficientwise map into a local power-series ring.

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tags 00HT and 00HR, in the coefficientwise specialization
of Tag 0523 (blueprint: lem-per-formal-coefficient-taylor-faithful-flatness).
The maximal ideal of the target is `(m_B, Z)`, equivalently the series with constant
coefficient in `m_B`. Its inverse image is prime. Every denominator outside that inverse
image maps to a unit, so `IsLocalization.lift` constructs the specified map, without choices
of an arbitrary homomorphism. On fractions it sends `F/G` to `map ρ F / map ρ G`.
-/
noncomputable def localCoefficientMap {A : Type u} {B : Type v}
    [CommRing A] [CommRing B] [IsLocalRing B] (ρ : A →+* B) (n : ℕ) :
    Localization.AtPrime
      ((IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal (MvPowerSeries (Fin n) B)).comap (map ρ)) →+*
        MvPowerSeries (Fin n) B :=
  IsLocalization.lift (g := map ρ)
    (M := ((IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal (MvPowerSeries (Fin n) B)).comap (map ρ)).primeCompl)
    (fun s ↦ IsLocalRing.notMem_maximalIdeal.mp s.property)

/-- Flat coefficientwise extension and its faithfully flat local form.

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 0523 (lemma-flat-module-powers), applied to formal
variables; Tags 0306, 00HT and 00HR for the other inputs
(blueprint: lem-per-formal-coefficient-taylor-faithful-flatness).

Proof:
1. Let `I` and `J` be the ideals generated by the variables over `A` and `B`. A series
   belongs to their kth powers exactly when its coefficients below total degree k vanish:
   multiplication gives one direction; for the converse group each monomial by one of the
   finitely many degree-k divisors. This is valid for zero variables as well.
2. The finite monomial bases identify `(R/I^k) ⊗[A] B` with `T/J^k`, compatibly with the
   coefficientwise map. Tensor associativity identifies tensoring a module injection over
   `R/I^k` with this ring with tensoring its underlying A-module injection with B. Flatness
   of ρ therefore proves flatness of every quotient map (def-weil-pbc-module-tensor-product
   and def-weil-pbc-flat-ring-map).
3. Every maximal ideal of T contains J. For h in J and arbitrary t, the geometric series
   of th exists coefficientwise and inverts `1-th`. If h escaped a maximal ideal, choose t
   representing its inverse in the residue field and obtain a unit in that ideal.
4. Both series rings are Noetherian by lem-weil-pbc-power-series-noetherian. Apply
   lem-weil-pbc-flat-module-powers with M = T, finite over itself, and the quotient flatness
   of step 2. It gives flatness of T localized at every maximal ideal by step 3. Then
   lem-sfb-base-flatness-maximal-localizations gives flatness of T over R, also when T = 0.
5. If B is nonzero local, lem-weil-pbc-power-series-local-ring identifies the maximal ideal
   of T with `(m_B, Z)`: subtracting the constant term gives the variable-ideal part.
   Its inverse image q is prime. By lem-weil-pbc-flat-local-ring-maps the map R_q to T_n
   is flat local; since every element outside n is a unit, T_n identifies canonically
   with T. The resulting map is `localCoefficientMap`, by the localization universal
   property. Apply lem-weil-pbc-flat-local-hom-faithfully-flat. For n = 0 the empty-variable
   equivalence identifies this with ρ and its indicated localization.
-/
theorem map_flat_and_local_faithfullyFlat {A : Type u} {B : Type v}
    [CommRing A] [CommRing B] [IsNoetherianRing A] [IsNoetherianRing B]
    (ρ : A →+* B) (hρ : ρ.Flat) (n : ℕ) :
    (map (σ := Fin n) ρ).Flat ∧
      ∀ [IsLocalRing B],
        IsLocalHom (localCoefficientMap ρ n) ∧
          @Module.FaithfullyFlat
            (Localization.AtPrime
              ((IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal (MvPowerSeries (Fin n) B)).comap (map ρ)))
            (MvPowerSeries (Fin n) B) _ _ (localCoefficientMap ρ n).toAlgebra.toModule := by
  sorry

end MvPowerSeries
